      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "fba0f0c17d39833f096b677777186700c8b8ce5691baaa90d5b25e7124fef31b"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "A more general procedure for estimating $ \\operatorname{Var}(t_{\\mathrm{PHS}}) $, which allows for unbalanced designs, is to use the large-sample estimator for the variance of a ratio given in Appendix 1. Such a computation requires estimates of the sampling variances and covariances of $ \\operatorname{Var}(s) $, $ \\operatorname{Var}(d) $, and $ \\operatorname{Var}(e) $, expressions for which are given in Hammond and Nicholas (1972) and Searle et al. (1992). If one is willing to assume normality and to ignore the sampling variance of $ \\operatorname{Var}(z) $ and the sampling covariance between $ \\operatorname{Var}(z) $, $ \\operatorname{Var}(s) $, and $ \\operatorname{Var}(d) $, some fairly simple and conservative (upwardly biased) estimates are possible,",
      "source_page": 591,
      "printed_page": 571,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter18:p025:b2"
      ],
      "bbox": [
